WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.540
Us, writer/director Jordan Peele's much-anticipated
follow-up to his horror masterpiece Get Out,

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.800
is finally here, and it was worth the wait.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.780
What starts as an apparently simple home invasion
fright fest quickly evolves into something

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:18.160
bigger, as Peele spins bigger and bigger ideas
into a movie with bigger and bigger implications.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.400
The result is a conceptually weighty horror
film that keeps you guessing and thinking

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.340
right up to the very last minute, and that
means we've got plenty to talk about.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.590
With that in mind, here are the best and worst
things about Us.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.940
Oh, and it should go without saying, but there
are SPOILERS AHEAD for the entire movie.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:34.800
You've been warned.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:40.260
Horror cinema has a huge capacity for pushing
actors to their emotional and physical limits,

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.960
and the genre's long tradition of placing
vulnerable women at the center of stories

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.720
means we've gotten truly legendary performances
out of some major talents, from Jamie Lee

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.280
Curtis to Sigourney Weaver to Toni Collette.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:56.780
Adelaide, played by Oscar-winner Lupita Nyong'o,
is the center of Us, and she rises to the

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:01.960
occasion by adding another legendary performance
to horror film history, a performance made

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.340
all the more powerful by her dual roles.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.680
As Adelaide she is vulnerable but strong,
scared but determined, loving but savage.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.780
As Red, Adelaide's Tethered double, she is
both an entirely different personality and

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.040
every bit Adelaide's mirror image.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:22.720
She jerks and twists when Adelaide is graceful,
grins when Adelaide gasps in terror, and croaks

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:23.740
where Adelaide speaks.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:25.960
"Be careful…"

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.179
It's a magnificent pair of performances, and
while there are certainly plenty of other

00:01:32.179 --> 00:01:36.619
things to recommend about Us, her daring and
brilliant performance is the first thing many

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:38.720
moviegoers will come away talking about.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.299
The Tyler family who join the Wilsons join
for a day at the beach in the first act of

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:47.740
the film, are presented as shallow, bickering
people with no real sense of love between

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:48.240
them.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.860
While that creates plenty of opportunities
for comedy, it does mean the blow is dulled

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.540
a little bit when their murders happen midway
through the movie.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.140
Yes, that sequence is funny and gets quite
tense when the Wilsons show up, but you get

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:04.049
the sense that the Tylers didn't really need
to be as broad as they are, particularly when

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:07.120
the film already roots us in the emotional
reality of the Wilsons.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:12.400
Before he was an acclaimed genius of horror,
Jordan Peele was an acclaimed genius of comedy,

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.060
thanks to his collaboration with Keegan Michael
Key, which resulted in the TV series Key &amp; Peele,

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.200
and their film Keanu.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.660
Peele never made some kind of hard cut from
one genre to the other, though.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:27.380
As a horror fan, he has an innate understanding
of the role comedy plays in scary movies,

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.700
something he demonstrated over and over again
with his debut horror feature, Get Out.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:32.700
"How you find me?"

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:38.780
"I'm TS motherf---ing A. We handle s---."

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:44.380
Us is another masterclass in balancing laughs
and scares, beginning with the interplay of

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:48.370
the Wilson family as they travel together
in the car and continuing throughout the entire

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:48.860
film.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.480
"What's I got five on it mean?"

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:52.480
"It's about drugs."

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.800
"It's not about drugs, it's a dope song.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:55.420
Don't do drugs."

00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:00.480
Like his horror, Peele's comedy starts small,
with little Dad jokes from Gabe and comforting

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.220
family banter.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:06.320
It continues to build from there until it's
intertwining with the scares, as Gabe wrestles

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.460
with his Tethered double on his new boat.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.060
Ultimately, in the most desperate situations
in the film, the comedy provides a welcome

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:17.630
sense of fun, as the Wilson family can't help
commenting on how bizarre their situation

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.380
is even as they're trying to survive it.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:24.540
All of that, plus a welcome needle drop from
NWA, means that Us is often as hilarious as

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:25.540
it is terrifying.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:30.200
Us begins with a relatively tight concept
- evil duplicates are invading, and trying

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.440
to kill… us!

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.340
"What are you people?"

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:38.600
"It's us."

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.980
As the film expands and expands, it keeps
expanding until it essentially has nationwide

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:46.720
implications, and the themes just keep expanding
with it.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:51.260
Because of that expansion, Us gets messy and
not just in terms of the body count.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:56.060
The film swells until it becomes not just
about our own dark sides, but about how some

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.040
people prosper while others suffer in an unfair
system.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.480
And how the powerful manipulate regular people
in unseen ways.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:06.680
And about empty gestures of charity and hope
that are mostly about public relations.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.060
And about the lengths we'll go to in order
to take what we feel we deserve… and so

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:11.800
on, and so on.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.860
These are all valid themes!

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.859
But by the end, the film tries to throw these
things at us all at once, and some of them

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:21.180
land hard while others simply fall by the
wayside.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:25.280
It's a shotgun approach to the symbolism at
the heart of the movie, and it doesn't work

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:26.599
as well it should overall.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:31.080
It could even be said that, without Get Out
to compare it to, we wouldn't notice the difference,

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:32.960
but it's there, and it's noticeable.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.460
Still, you gotta give the movie credit: even
if its reach exceeds its grasp, at least it

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:38.460
tried to reach at all.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:43.600
With Us, Jordan Peele created some legendary
creepy nightmares called the Tethered, and

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.400
they live up to all the promise they had in
the film's trailers.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:49.220
"Stick with me, and I'll keep you safe."

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:50.060
"Run!"

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.240
The design of the Tethered is deceptively
simple, and even though we don't learn why

00:04:55.259 --> 00:04:59.580
they look the way they do, red jumpsuits,
gloves, sandals, and scissors, until late

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:03.949
in the film, their look works from the very
beginning, when we see them standing in the

00:05:03.949 --> 00:05:06.280
Wilson family's driveway, silent and stoic.

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:11.520
As the concept at the movie's heart continues
expanding, and its mythology gets more complex,

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.020
the Tethered themselves remain deceptively
simple movie monsters.

00:05:15.449 --> 00:05:19.720
Their behavior can change a bit and their
motives can become more clear, but the deliberate,

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:24.060
almost robotic way in which they pursue their
goals does not, and that makes them terrifying.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:28.800
They're extremely effective movie monsters
thanks in part to Peele's less-is-more approach,

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:32.320
and the actors inhabiting them make them unforgettable
slasher villains.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:38.000
As the film's final act leads Adelaide down
into a series of tunnels, we finally learn

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.610
what the Tethered really are: genetic copies
of people living on the surface world, human

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:46.770
in body but vacant in spirit, who were created
to serve as underground "puppets" for the

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:48.500
powerful to manipulate the citizenry.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:52.960
It eventually all went wrong, and the Tethered
were simply left in the tunnels to fend for

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.660
themselves… until Red herself began to organize
the uprising we see in the film.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:02.879
It's an intriguing explanation for a lot of
seemingly unrelated things, but the way it's

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.980
finally revealed to us, with Red explaining
it all via voiceover, could've been better.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.889
We've seen this villain do all manner of frightening,
unpredictable, and compelling things, and

00:06:11.889 --> 00:06:16.240
now in this moment she's reduced to monologuing
in a dark room because the film has to dish

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.880
out a whole bunch of information in a very
short time.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.360
It's perhaps the one inelegant thing the film
has to offer, and it still manages to be interesting

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:25.360
even if it's clunky.

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:30.360
Peele is such a talented writer, skilled at
wielding high concepts in new ways, mixing

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:34.289
comedy and drama, and pacing in a way that
makes it look easy, that he could shoot his

00:06:34.289 --> 00:06:37.740
films in a style that's merely competent and
still come away with hits.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:41.680
As long as everything's in focus and the editing
isn't confusing, the story will still work

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:43.340
and the characters will still come through.

00:06:43.689 --> 00:06:47.840
Peele isn't content to just keep things adequate,
though, and that shows through in the visual

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.780
flourishes present throughout Us.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:51.700
Every frame of this film looks amazing.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:56.000
It doesn't matter if you're a fan of horror
or not, this is some of the finest visual

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.960
storytelling you're likely to see in any movie
in 2019.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:03.900
The eventual explanation for who and what
the Tethered really are and why they were

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:08.930
created is an intriguing one, and helps to
set up that amazing final shot of bloody Hands

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:09.720
Across America.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:14.020
But things start to fall apart a bit the more
you think about the logistics of it all: Why

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:17.280
was the entrance to the Tethered's underground
tunnels unlocked?

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:20.360
Why didn't more wander to the surface world
more often?

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.920
Why can the Tethered act with free will in
some scenes and become mirror images of their

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:26.020
counterparts the next?

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:30.620
There are probably great explanations for
these questions, but the film is too interested

00:07:30.629 --> 00:07:34.849
in moving relentlessly forward to give them
to us, and that might take a little air out

00:07:34.849 --> 00:07:38.000
of the story's tires if you get stuck thinking
too much about it all.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:42.430
On that same subject, it's confirmed in the
movie's final moments that Adelaide is actually

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:47.849
the original Tethered, and Red was the original
Adelaide who she strangled and locked up in

00:07:47.849 --> 00:07:51.100
the tunnels after that first carnival encounter
all those years ago.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:54.840
The twist works, because it's the kind of
thing that just burrows into your brain as

00:07:54.849 --> 00:07:59.389
the credits are rolling… but it also feels
like the last brick that almost makes the

00:07:59.389 --> 00:08:01.020
tower beneath it start to topple.

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:05.200
If there's one impulse the movie maybe should
have resisted, it might be this one…

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:09.860
unless we get to explore the true implications
of this revelation in the inevitable sequel,

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:12.200
Us, Too: A Bus Full of Us!

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:14.380
Now that sounds scary...

